%T A402369 1574042841,1574285302,1574304473,1574304984,2544832187,2798034948,
%U A402369 2891899069,2922174980,2930299551,2931990532,2932232993,2932252164,2932252675,3410614459,3504478580,3534754491,3542879062,3544570043
%N A402369 Numbers that are the sum of nine distinct positive 9th powers.
%e A402369 a(1) = Sum_{k=1..9} k^9 = A031971(9) is clearly the smallest such number.
%e A402369 a(2) = Sum_{k=1..8} k^9 + 10^9 is the next larger such number, then
%e A402369 a(3) = Sum_{k=1..10} k^9 - 8^9 (i.e., 8^9 is omitted in the sum),
%e A402369 a(4) = Sum_{k=1..10} k^9 - 7^9 (i.e., 7^9 is omitted in the sum), ...
%e A402369 a(9) = Sum_{k=1..10} k^9 - 2^9 (i.e., 2^9 is omitted in the sum),
%e A402369 a(10) = Sum_{k=2..10} k^9.
%e A402369 We see that the first 10 terms are a(n) = a(10)+1 - (11-n)^9, 1 <= n <= 10.
%e A402369 Then the sequence goes on with 2 omitted terms among {k^9, 1 <= k <= 11}, then with 3 omitted terms among {k^9, 1 <= k <= 12}, etc. This pattern breaks when m^9 < Sum_{k=m-9..m-1} k^9, which happens for m = 15. From there on, sums with largest term 15^9 may be smaller than than sums with largest term 14^9, and similar for larger m.
%o A402369 (PARI) A402369_first(n=50, k=9)=my(L=List(),m=k); while(#L<n^1.05, forvec(v=vector(k,i,[m^(i==k),m]), listput(L,sum(i=1,k,v[i]^k)), 2); m++); Set(L)[1..n] \\ also equals A402364_row(9, len=n).
%Y A402369 Cf. A001017 (ninth powers), A031971 (Sum k^n, k=1..n).
%Y A402369 Row 9 of A402364 (n-th row lists sums of n distinct n-th powers > 0).
%Y A402369 Similar sequences of numbers that are the sum of k distinct positive k-th powers: A004431 (k=2), A024975 (k=3), A176197 (k=4), A402365 (k=5), A402366 (k=6), A402367 (k=7), A402368 (k=8).
%K A402369 nonn,new
